insert into public.content_bodies (content_id, body_md, body_html)
select id, '
Calculus is the mathematics of change. Speed is the change in distance; acceleration is the change in speed; the area under a curve adds up infinitely many tiny changes. This short ebook gives you the core ideas in a weekend — intuition first, formulas second.

<!-- more -->

## Chapter 1 · Limits

A **limit** asks: *what value does $f(x)$ approach as $x$ gets closer and closer to some number?* We write

$$
\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L
$$

The function does not even need to be defined at $a$. A classic example:

$$
\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sin x}{x} = 1
$$

Plug in $x = 0$ and you get $\tfrac{0}{0}$, which is meaningless — yet values like $x = 0.01$ give $0.99998\ldots$. The limit captures where the function is *heading*.

> [!NOTE]
> Limits are the foundation everything else is built on. If a definition later looks scary, look for the limit hiding inside it.

## Chapter 2 · Derivatives

The **derivative** measures instantaneous rate of change — the slope of the curve at a single point:

$$
f''(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}
$$

You rarely compute it from the definition. A handful of rules cover most cases:

| Rule | Formula |
|---|---|
| Power | $\dfrac{d}{dx} x^n = n x^{n-1}$ |
| Sum | $(f + g)'' = f'' + g''$ |
| Product | $(fg)'' = f''g + fg''$ |
| Chain | $\dfrac{d}{dx} f(g(x)) = f''(g(x))\, g''(x)$ |

**Example.** If a ball''s height is $h(t) = 20t - 5t^2$ metres, its velocity is $h''(t) = 20 - 10t$. It stops rising when $h''(t) = 0$, at $t = 2$ seconds, reaching $h(2) = 20$ metres.

## Chapter 3 · Integrals

An **integral** adds up infinitely many infinitely thin slices. The definite integral is the signed area under a curve:

$$
\int_a^b f(x)\,dx
$$

The **Fundamental Theorem of Calculus** connects integrals to derivatives: if $F''(x) = f(x)$, then

$$
\int_a^b f(x)\,dx = F(b) - F(a)
$$

**Example.** The area under $y = x^2$ from 0 to 3:

$$
\int_0^3 x^2\,dx = \left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_0^3 = \frac{27}{3} - 0 = 9
$$

## Chapter 4 · Applications

**Optimization.** You have 20 m of fence and want the largest rectangular pen. With width $x$, the length is $10 - x$ and the area is

$$
A(x) = x(10 - x) = 10x - x^2, \qquad A''(x) = 10 - 2x
$$

Setting $A''(x) = 0$ gives $x = 5$: a $5 \times 5$ square with area $25\text{ m}^2$.

**Motion.** Integrate velocity to get distance travelled; differentiate position to get velocity. The two operations undo each other.

## Chapter 5 · Where to go next

- Practise derivatives until the rules are automatic.
- Learn **u-substitution** and **integration by parts** — the integral versions of the chain and product rules.
- Explore **differential equations**, which describe everything from population growth to circuits.

> [!TIP]
> Fifteen minutes a day beats a three-hour cram. Calculus rewards repetition.
', '<p>Calculus is the mathematics of change. Speed is the change in distance; acceleration is the change in speed; the area under a curve adds up infinitely many tiny changes. This short ebook gives you the core ideas in a weekend — intuition first, formulas second.</p>
<!-- more -->
<h2 id="chapter-1-limits">Chapter 1 · Limits</h2>
<p>A <strong>limit</strong> asks: <em>what value does <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span> approach as <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></span> gets closer and closer to some number?</em> We write</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>lim</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></munder><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x \to a} f(x) = L</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p>The function does not even need to be defined at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a</annotation></semantics></math></span>. A classic example:</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><munder><mrow><mi>lim</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>sin</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mi>x</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lim_{x \to 0} \frac{\sin x}{x} = 1</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p>Plug in <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> and you get <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mn>0</mn><mn>0</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tfrac{0}{0}</annotation></semantics></math></span>, which is meaningless — yet values like <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.01</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x = 0.01</annotation></semantics></math></span> give <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.99998</mn><mo>…</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">0.99998\ldots</annotation></semantics></math></span>. The limit captures where the function is <em>heading</em>.</p>
<blockquote class="callout callout-note">
<p class="callout-title">Note</p>
<p>Limits are the foundation everything else is built on. If a definition later looks scary, look for the limit hiding inside it.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2 id="chapter-2-derivatives">Chapter 2 · Derivatives</h2>
<p>The <strong>derivative</strong> measures instantaneous rate of change — the slope of the curve at a single point:</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><munder><mrow><mi>lim</mi><mo>⁡</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo>→</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></munder><mfrac><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mi>h</mi></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f&#x27;(x) = \lim_{h \to 0} \frac{f(x+h) - f(x)}{h}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p>You rarely compute it from the definition. A handful of rules cover most cases:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Rule</th>
<th>Formula</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Power</td>
<td><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{d}{dx} x^n = n x^{n-1}</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Sum</td>
<td><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>g</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>g</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f + g)&#x27; = f&#x27; + g&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Product</td>
<td><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>f</mi><mi>g</mi><msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mi>g</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>f</mi><msup><mi>g</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(fg)&#x27; = f&#x27;g + fg&#x27;</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Chain</td>
<td><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel="0" displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>d</mi><mrow><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><msup><mi>g</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dfrac{d}{dx} f(g(x)) = f&#x27;(g(x))\, g&#x27;(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span></td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p><strong>Example.</strong> If a ball’s height is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mi>t</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(t) = 20t - 5t^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> metres, its velocity is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&#x27;(t) = 20 - 10t</annotation></semantics></math></span>. It stops rising when <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>h</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h&#x27;(t) = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span>, at <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t = 2</annotation></semantics></math></span> seconds, reaching <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>20</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">h(2) = 20</annotation></semantics></math></span> metres.</p>
<h2 id="chapter-3-integrals">Chapter 3 · Integrals</h2>
<p>An <strong>integral</strong> adds up infinitely many infinitely thin slices. The definite integral is the signed area under a curve:</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_a^b f(x)\,dx</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p>The <strong>Fundamental Theorem of Calculus</strong> connects integrals to derivatives: if <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>F</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F&#x27;(x) = f(x)</annotation></semantics></math></span>, then</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>b</mi></msubsup><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>−</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_a^b f(x)\,dx = F(b) - F(a)</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p><strong>Example.</strong> The area under <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>=</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">y = x^2</annotation></semantics></math></span> from 0 to 3:</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mo>∫</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mtext> </mtext><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo fence="true">[</mo><mfrac><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo fence="true">]</mo></mrow><mn>0</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>27</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\int_0^3 x^2\,dx = \left[\frac{x^3}{3}\right]_0^3 = \frac{27}{3} - 0 = 9</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<h2 id="chapter-4-applications">Chapter 4 · Applications</h2>
<p><strong>Optimization.</strong> You have 20 m of fence and want the largest rectangular pen. With width <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math></span>, the length is <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10 - x</annotation></semantics></math></span> and the area is</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>−</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo separator="true">,</mo><mspace width="2em"/><msup><mi>A</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A(x) = x(10 - x) = 10x - x^2, \qquad A&#x27;(x) = 10 - 2x</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p>Setting <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo mathvariant="normal" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">′</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A&#x27;(x) = 0</annotation></semantics></math></span> gives <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x = 5</annotation></semantics></math></span>: a <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5 \times 5</annotation></semantics></math></span> square with area <span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"><semantics><mrow><mn>25</mn><msup><mtext> m</mtext><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">25\text{ m}^2</annotation></semantics></math></span>.</p>
<p><strong>Motion.</strong> Integrate velocity to get distance travelled; differentiate position to get velocity. The two operations undo each other.</p>
<h2 id="chapter-5-where-to-go-next">Chapter 5 · Where to go next</h2>
<ul>
<li>Practise derivatives until the rules are automatic.</li>
<li>Learn <strong>u-substitution</strong> and <strong>integration by parts</strong> — the integral versions of the chain and product rules.</li>
<li>Explore <strong>differential equations</strong>, which describe everything from population growth to circuits.</li>
</ul>
<blockquote class="callout callout-tip">
<p class="callout-title">Tip</p>
<p>Fifteen minutes a day beats a three-hour cram. Calculus rewards repetition.</p>
</blockquote>
