<h2 id="1-the-staff-and-the-treble-clef">1. The staff and the treble clef</h2>
<p>Music is written on a <strong>staff</strong> of five lines. The swirly <strong>treble clef</strong> at the start wraps around the second line from the bottom, marking it as the note <strong>G</strong>.</p>
<ul>
<li>Notes on the <strong>lines</strong>, bottom to top: <strong>E G B D F</strong> — “Every Good Boy Does Fine”.</li>
<li>Notes in the <strong>spaces</strong>, bottom to top: <strong>F A C E</strong> — it spells <em>face</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p>Middle C sits on its own short line (a <em>ledger line</em>) just below the staff.</p>
<h2 id="2-rhythm-how-long-each-note-lasts">2. Rhythm: how long each note lasts</h2>
<p>In the most common time signature, <strong>4/4</strong>, each bar holds four beats:</p>
<div class="table-wrap"><table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Note</th>
<th>Beats in 4/4</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td>Whole note (hollow, no stem)</td>
<td>4</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Half note (hollow, with stem)</td>
<td>2</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Quarter note (filled, with stem)</td>
<td>1</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Eighth note (filled, with a flag)</td>
<td>½</td>
</tr>
</tbody>
</table></div>
<p>The top number of the time signature says how many beats are in a bar; the bottom number says which note gets one beat (4 = quarter note).</p>
<h2 id="3-read-the-melody">3. Read the melody</h2>
<p>Go back to <em>Twinkle, Twinkle</em> above and read it bar by bar:</p>
<ol>
<li>C C G G — four quarter notes</li>
<li>A A G — two quarters, then a <strong>half note</strong> held for two beats</li>
<li>F F E E</li>
<li>D D C — ending on a half note</li>
</ol>
<blockquote class="callout callout-tip">
<p class="callout-title">Tip</p>
<p>Clap the rhythm first, then say the note names, and only then play. Separating the two skills makes both easier.</p>
</blockquote>
<h2 id="4-a-scale-to-practise">4. A scale to practise</h2>
