insert into public.content_bodies (content_id, body_md, body_html)
select id, '
Sunlight looks white, but it is a mix of every colour of the rainbow. On its way through the atmosphere it bumps into nitrogen and oxygen molecules that are far smaller than the wavelength of light — and that is where the blue comes from.

<!-- more -->

## Rayleigh scattering

Small particles scatter short wavelengths much more strongly than long ones. The amount of scattering is proportional to one over the wavelength to the fourth power:

$$
I \propto \frac{1}{\lambda^4}
$$

Blue light (about 450 nm) versus red light (about 700 nm):

$$
\left(\frac{700}{450}\right)^4 \approx 5.9
$$

So blue light is scattered roughly six times more than red. Wherever you look in the sky, scattered blue light is coming towards you.

## Then why isn''t the sky violet?

Violet light has an even shorter wavelength, but:

- the Sun emits less violet than blue,
- some violet is absorbed high in the atmosphere, and
- our eyes are much more sensitive to blue.

## Red sunsets

At sunset, light travels through far more air to reach you. Most of the blue is scattered away along the path, leaving the oranges and reds.

> [!NOTE]
> On Mars the effect is reversed: fine dust makes the daytime sky butterscotch and sunsets bluish.
', '<p>Sunlight looks white, but it is a mix of every colour of the rainbow. On its way through the atmosphere it bumps into nitrogen and oxygen molecules that are far smaller than the wavelength of light — and that is where the blue comes from.</p>
<!-- more -->
<h2 id="rayleigh-scattering">Rayleigh scattering</h2>
<p>Small particles scatter short wavelengths much more strongly than long ones. The amount of scattering is proportional to one over the wavelength to the fourth power:</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>∝</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msup><mi>λ</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I \propto \frac{1}{\lambda^4}</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p>Blue light (about 450 nm) versus red light (about 700 nm):</p>
<div class="math-display"><span class="katex"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo fence="true">(</mo><mfrac><mn>700</mn><mn>450</mn></mfrac><mo fence="true">)</mo></mrow><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>≈</mo><mn>5.9</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\left(\frac{700}{450}\right)^4 \approx 5.9</annotation></semantics></math></span></div>
<p>So blue light is scattered roughly six times more than red. Wherever you look in the sky, scattered blue light is coming towards you.</p>
<h2 id="then-why-isn-t-the-sky-violet">Then why isn’t the sky violet?</h2>
<p>Violet light has an even shorter wavelength, but:</p>
<ul>
<li>the Sun emits less violet than blue,</li>
<li>some violet is absorbed high in the atmosphere, and</li>
<li>our eyes are much more sensitive to blue.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="red-sunsets">Red sunsets</h2>
<p>At sunset, light travels through far more air to reach you. Most of the blue is scattered away along the path, leaving the oranges and reds.</p>
<blockquote class="callout callout-note">
